    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d140">

                <pb facs="0250" n="148" sameAs="frus:frus1933v03/pg148"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/5866</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chinese Ministry of Foreign
                            Affairs to the Chinese
                        Legation</hi><anchor n="72" xml:id="d140-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1933-02-04">February 4, 1933</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>A spokesman of the Chinese Foreign Office denied categorically the report that
                    Great Britain had proposed to the Chinese Government, through Sir Miles Lampson,
                    the British Minister to China, a round table conference at either Nanking or
                    Shanghai for the settlement of the Sino-Japanese dispute, declaring that the
                    report must have emanated from Japanese sources. The circulation of such reports
                    on the eve of the League’s preparing verdict on the Manchurian question serves
                    only to show the extreme desperation and nervousness of the Japanese at the
                    prospect of a strong report in condemnation of Japan’s unlawful activities in
                    Manchuria, the spokesman added.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
